@database "json_store"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: json_store.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "JSON Store"
@{h1}JSON Store

A place for agents to keep JSON documents while they work. An agent that
builds a large JSON result over many steps -- collecting sub-agent answers,
filling in one field after another -- breaks it when it types the whole
document again each time: values get shortened, escapes go missing, commas
vanish. With this plugin the document lives on the server as data. The agent
sends pieces, the plugin checks and repairs them, merges them in code, and
every read returns valid JSON. Several agents can share documents through a
common namespace, each document can only be changed by the agent that
created it, the last few changes can be undone, and everything is saved to
disk so an aborted run can continue. It has no panel.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The tool  " link tool}
  @{"  Paths, merging and undo  " link rules}
  @{"  Namespaces and ownership  " link sharing}
  @{"  Key models  " link keymodels}
  @{"  Where the data lives  " link storage}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is not in the default @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}. Each agent
that needs a store declares its own instance in its YAML, next to the agent.
The workflow agent (@{tt}config/agents/workflow_agent.yaml@{utt}) has this
one:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:

    # Validated JSON working documents for the workflow agent
    wf_json:
      type: json_store
      enabled: true
      description: "workflow_agent JSON documents (validated, code-side merge)"
      config:
        max_docs: 100
        max_doc_bytes: 250000
@{body}

and allows it in the agent's tool list:

@{code yaml}
        tools:
          allowed:
            - "wf_json/*"
@{body}

The tool is then @{tt}wf_json_manage_json@{utt}: the instance name comes
first. Several instances are separate stores with separate directories. No
extra packages.

@{h2}Server settings

The settings go under @{tt}config:@{utt} in the server entry, as above --
directly in the entry the plugin does not see them. They are read once, when
the instance starts.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
session_scoped | true | a call without namespace uses the caller's session; false: all such calls share one space, "global"
require_namespace | false | true: every call without namespace is refused (for stores that exist only to be shared)
default_write_access | owner | owner or shared, for documents created without write_access; any other value makes every create fail
max_docs | 50 | documents per namespace
max_doc_bytes | 2097152 | size limit of one document, in characters of its compact JSON
undo_depth | 5 | changes per document that undo can revert; 0 turns undo off
namespace_ttl_hours | 48 | a namespace nobody touched this long is dropped from memory (reloaded from disk when used again); 0: never
persist | true | save every document to disk; false: memory only
storage_path | data/json_store | root directory; the instance name and the namespace are added below it
max_read_chars | 50000 | largest read answer, in characters of the indented JSON; when the entry sets max_doc_bytes and not this, the default is twice max_doc_bytes, so a store sized for its documents reads them whole; a number below 1 or not a number means that default
file_retention_hours | 336 | at startup, a namespace directory whose newest file is older than this is deleted; 0: keep forever
key_models | none | allowed keys and types per document name; see @{" Key models " link keymodels}
key_aliases | none | fixed renames of wrong keys, used with key_models
@{body}

With a data directory configured (@{tt}paths.data_dir@{utt} or
@{tt}AGENT_DATA_DIR@{utt}), the default storage path lies in it. A relative
@{tt}storage_path@{utt} you set yourself counts from the directory the
process was started in.
@endnode

@node tool "The tool"
@{h1}The tool

One tool, @{tt}<instance>_manage_json@{utt}, with the action chosen by
@{tt}operation@{utt}.

@{h2}Operations

@{table}
operation | Does
write | create a document; with if_exists replace, overwrite one
read | the document, or the part at path, as JSON text
merge | merge data into a document, creating it if it is missing
merge_doc | merge the stored document source into doc
set_value | set one value at path, creating what is missing on the way
delete_keys | remove the given paths from a document
delete_doc | remove a whole document
undo | revert the last change to a document
list | the documents of the namespace
outline | the document's structure: keys and types, no values
stats | terms that recur across the children of a part of the document
@{body}

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Used by | Meaning
operation | all | required; an unknown one answers with the list of valid ones
namespace | all | the shared space to work in; without it, the caller's own; see @{" Namespaces and ownership " link sharing}
doc | all but list | the document name; for write it may be left out, then the plugin makes a new name like doc_3f9a1c07 and returns it; for merge_doc the target
source | merge_doc | the document merged from; must differ from doc
data | write, merge | the JSON as a value (preferred); wins when json_text is sent too
json_text | write, merge | the JSON as text; repaired if needed, see below
if_exists | write | error (default): a named document that exists is refused; replace: overwrite it
write_access | write, merge, merge_doc, set_value | only when the call creates the document: owner or shared; default default_write_access
path | read, set_value, stats | where in the document; see @{" Paths, merging and undo " link rules}; stats: empty means the whole document
value | set_value | required; any JSON value, null included
paths | delete_keys | required, a non-empty list of paths
array_mode | merge, merge_doc | replace (default): a list overwrites a list; concat: appended
depth | outline | how deep, default 3; below 0 counts as 0
min_children | stats | a term must occur in this many children, default 2; values below 2 count as 2
top | stats | how many terms, default 12 (also for 0)
exclude | stats | terms to leave out: a list, or one comma-separated text; an entry matches as part of a term, ignoring case; entries under 3 characters are ignored
@{body}

A parameter of the wrong type (a number as @{tt}doc@{utt}, "abc" as
@{tt}depth@{utt}) answers "Invalid parameter for 'outline': ...".

@{h2}JSON sent as text

@{tt}json_text@{utt} is read as follows: a Markdown code fence around it is
removed; then it is parsed. If that fails, a repair library tries again
(raw line breaks in strings, missing or extra commas, single quotes,
missing closing brackets). A repair is only accepted when it gives an
object or a list; otherwise the answer is "Invalid JSON at line L column C:
... Auto-repair could not recover a JSON object/array — check for
truncation or non-JSON content, then resend." What was done is listed in
the answer's @{tt}repairs@{utt}: @{tt}stripped_code_fence@{utt},
@{tt}auto_repaired_json@{utt}. A repair after a cut-off answer closes the
brackets, so the document may be shorter than intended -- the answer's
@{tt}chars@{utt} shows it.

@{tt}data@{utt} is taken as it is. A text sent as @{tt}data@{utt} is
stored as a text, not parsed.

@{h2}Answers

Every answer has @{tt}status@{utt}, ok or error; an error has
@{tt}error@{utt} with the reason and changes nothing.

@{table}
operation | Fields besides status
write | doc, chars (size of the compact JSON), top_level (the keys, at most 15 and then "+N more"; "array[N]"; or the type), replaced; repairs, remapped, persist_error when they apply
merge | as write, with created and merged_keys (the incoming top-level keys) instead of replaced
merge_doc | as merge, with merged_from
set_value | doc, chars, top_level, set_path
delete_keys | doc, chars, top_level, deleted, missing (the paths that were not there)
delete_doc | deleted (the name), remaining (the names left); persist_error when the file could not be removed
undo | doc, undone (the operation reverted), restored, chars, top_level, snapshots_left; or restored false and deleted true when the undone change had created the document; persist_error when saving or removing the file failed
read | doc, path, chars (of the returned text), json (the JSON, indented by 2, non-ASCII kept)
list | count, docs (doc, chars, writable, top_level each)
outline | doc, outline
stats | doc, path, n_children, min_children, recurring_terms (term and children each)
@{body}

A read whose text would be longer than @{tt}max_read_chars@{utt} is
refused, never cut: "'plan' is 81234 chars, over the read limit of 50000.
Read a part with 'path'; 'outline' shows the structure." The same limit
holds for an outline: "Outline of 'plan' at depth 3 is 81234 chars, over
the read limit of 50000. Use a smaller 'depth'."

An unknown document answers "Document 'x' not found. Did you mean 'y'?
Existing: [...]" with the fields @{tt}did_you_mean@{utt} and
@{tt}existing@{utt} (up to 15 names).

@{h2}The outline

Objects show their keys, texts their length (@{tt}string(1200)@{utt}),
other values their type (@{tt}int@{utt}, @{tt}float@{utt}, @{tt}bool@{utt},
@{tt}null@{utt}). Of a list only the first element is shown, followed by
"... +N more". Below the depth, objects become "object(N keys)" and lists
"array(N)".

@{h2}Recurring terms

@{tt}stats@{utt} looks at the node at @{tt}path@{utt}: its values when it
is an object, its elements when it is a list. For each child it collects
all texts inside it, splits them into words of 4 letters or more
(lowercase, digits dropped), leaves out a short built-in list of common
English and German filler words, and adds each pair of neighbouring words.
A term counts once per child. The answer lists the terms found in at least
@{tt}min_children@{utt} children, the most widespread first; a single word
is dropped when a pair containing it has the same count. Useful to see
which phrases a set of drafts keeps repeating.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"operation": "write", "namespace": "plan-42", "doc": "plan", "data": {"title": "Launch", "steps": []}}
{"operation": "set_value", "namespace": "plan-42", "doc": "plan", "path": "steps[0]", "value": {"label": "Draft"}}
{"operation": "read", "namespace": "plan-42", "doc": "plan", "path": "steps[0].label"}
@{body}
@endnode

@node rules "Paths, merging and undo"
@{h1}Paths, merging and undo

@{h2}Paths

A path names keys with dots and list positions in brackets:
@{tt}milestones[2].label@{utt}, @{tt}[0].x@{utt}. A plain number also
works as a list position (@{tt}items.0@{utt}). Empty parts are skipped. A
key that contains a dot or a bracket cannot be reached by a path.

@{bullet}@{b}read@{ub} of a path that is not there answers "Path segment
'x' not found — did you mean 'y'? (available keys: [...])".
@{bullet}@{b}set_value@{ub} creates what is missing: an object for a name,
a list for a bracket. A missing document is created as an object. On a list
the position may be one past the end, which appends. A value in the way
that is neither object nor list is replaced by one -- @{tt}a.x.y@{utt}
turns @{tt}"x": 1@{utt} into @{tt}"x": {"y": ...}@{utt}. A bracket on an
object is refused ("Path expects an array at '[0]' but found an object").
@{bullet}@{b}delete_keys@{ub} applies the paths one after another. Removing
a list element moves the later ones up: @{tt}["items[0]", "items[1]"]@{utt}
removes the first and the originally third element.

@{h2}Merging

@{tt}merge@{utt} and @{tt}merge_doc@{utt} work the same way: objects are
merged key by key, all the way down; any other value overwrites. Two lists
are replaced, or joined with @{tt}array_mode: concat@{utt}. When one side
is an object and the other is not, the merge is refused ("Root type
mismatch ... use write to replace"), because the document would be lost.
A merge into a document that does not exist creates it. @{tt}merge_doc@{utt}
copies the source, so the two documents are independent afterwards; both
must be in the same namespace.

@{h2}The checks every change passes

Before a write, merge, merge_doc or set_value is stored, in this order: the
caller may change the document (see @{" Namespaces and ownership " link sharing});
a new document fits under @{tt}max_docs@{utt} ("Too many documents (50).
Delete unused ones."); the key model accepts it; the result fits under
@{tt}max_doc_bytes@{utt} ("Document too large (N chars > limit M).").
A refused change leaves the stored document as it was.

@{h2}Undo

Before each change the plugin keeps a copy of the document.
@{tt}undo@{utt} puts the last copy back; call it again to go further, up to
@{tt}undo_depth@{utt} (5) steps. It reverts every kind of change; undoing
the change that created a document removes it, undoing a delete_doc brings
the document back with its owner. A change that changed nothing (the same
value again, delete_keys that found nothing) takes no step. Only the
document's owner may undo, and on a shared document anyone -- also someone
else's change. The copies are kept in memory only: a restart, or a
namespace dropped for being idle, loses them ("Nothing to undo for 'x' (no
recorded history)."). When another process changed the document, the
copies are dropped too; see @{" Where the data lives " link storage}.
@endnode

@node sharing "Namespaces and ownership"
@{h1}Namespaces and ownership

@{h2}Which namespace a call uses

@{bullet}With @{tt}namespace@{utt}: that one. Any agent, in any session,
that passes the same text sees the same documents -- this is how a
coordinator and its sub-agents share them.
@{bullet}Without it: the caller's session. A sub-agent has a session of its
own, so it does not see its coordinator's documents unless both pass the
same namespace.
@{bullet}Without it and with @{tt}session_scoped: false@{utt}, or when the
call has no session: the common namespace @{tt}global@{utt}.
@{bullet}With @{tt}require_namespace: true@{utt}, a call without it is
refused: "'namespace' is required on this store — pass the shared store id
you were given (the same value on every call). Without it your documents
land in a private, per-session space that nobody else can read."

@{h2}Who may change a document

A document belongs to the session that created it (@{tt}write_access:
owner@{utt}, the default). Everyone in the namespace may read it; changes
by another session are refused: "Document 'x' belongs to another agent and
is read-only for you. Read it freely, but do not modify it: write your own
document instead (omit 'doc' to get a fresh id) and let its owner merge
your changes." The usual way: each helper writes its own document without
a name, reports the returned name, and the owner merges it with
@{tt}merge_doc@{utt}. @{tt}list@{utt} shows for each document whether the
caller may change it (@{tt}writable@{utt}).

A document created with @{tt}write_access: shared@{utt} may be changed by
anyone in the namespace. The owner is the session the framework puts into
the call; a model or a script cannot pass another one, the framework drops
it. The owner is saved with the document, so it still holds after a
restart.
@endnode

@node keymodels "Key models"
@{h1}Key models

A key model fixes which keys a document may have and what type a value
must be. It is set per document name under @{tt}key_models@{utt}; an entry
named @{tt}"*"@{utt} applies to every document without its own, including
the ones with generated names.

@{code yaml}
config:
  key_models:
    "*":
      title: string
      themes: array
      notes: {}                     # anything
      milestones:
        - {label: string, date: string}
      people:
        "*": {role: string, age: {}}
  key_aliases:
    summary: summary_text
@{body}

@{table}
Model node | Means
an object of keys | only these keys; "*" among them allows any other key, modelled by its value
{} | anything below
a type name | the value must be string, number, integer, boolean, array, object, null or any; null always passes; an unknown name allows anything
a list with one model | the value must be a list; each element follows the model
@{body}

A key that is not allowed is renamed when it can be: first by
@{tt}key_aliases@{utt}, then when it is very close to an allowed key
(@{tt}genere@{utt} to @{tt}genre@{utt}), unless that key is already there.
The answer lists every rename in @{tt}remapped@{utt}. Anything else refuses
the whole change: "Key model violation(s): 'x' (did you mean 'y'?); 'z'
must be string but is array. Rename the flagged key ..." (up to 10 listed).
The model is checked on write, merge, merge_doc and set_value, always on
the whole resulting document; delete_keys and undo are not checked.
@endnode

@node storage "Where the data lives"
@{h1}Where the data lives

@{h2}In memory

Each instance keeps its documents in the memory of the process that runs
it, per namespace. The API and a command-line run are different processes
with different memory; they meet on disk (below).

@{h2}On disk

With @{tt}persist@{utt} on, every change writes the touched document to
@{tt}<storage_path>/<instance>/<namespace>/<doc>.json@{utt}, holding the
names, the owner and the data. A namespace that is not in memory is read
from there on first use -- so a run that was aborted and continued, or a
restarted server, finds its documents again. A document that cannot be
saved stays in memory, and the answer says so in @{tt}persist_error@{utt}.

Names are made safe for the file system: anything but letters, digits,
@{tt}_ . -@{utt} is replaced, and a name that had to change, has capitals,
ends in a dot, is a reserved Windows name or is longer than 60 characters
gets a short hash of the original. A namespace or document name cannot
reach outside the storage directory, and two names never share a file.

On Windows a file another process is reading cannot be replaced or
deleted, and a read during a replace fails for a moment; there the plugin
retries saving, reading and deleting for up to two seconds. On other
systems such an error is a real permission problem and is reported at
once. A file that could not be deleted keeps the document deleted in this
process, and the answer carries @{tt}persist_error@{utt}; after a restart
the document is back.

Files saved under an older naming rule -- a name ending in a dot, whose
folder Windows shared with the name without the dot -- are moved to their
current name the first time the namespace is loaded. When a file exists
under both names, the one written later is kept.

@{h2}Two processes on one store

Before every call the plugin compares the namespace's files with what it
last loaded or saved itself (identity, time and size of each file). A file another
process wrote is loaded again, with its owner; a file another process
deleted removes the document here too. A document loaded again this way
loses its undo copies -- they describe a state that is no longer the one on
disk. So the API and a command-line run on the same namespace see each
other's changes. Two changes to the same document at the same moment are
not merged: the one saved last wins.

@{h2}How long it stays

@{bullet}@{b}Memory@{ub}: a namespace nobody used for
@{tt}namespace_ttl_hours@{utt} (48) is dropped from memory at the next call
to the instance; its files stay. Without persistence this deletes the
documents.
@{bullet}@{b}Disk@{ub}: when an instance starts, it deletes every namespace
directory whose newest file is older than @{tt}file_retention_hours@{utt}
(14 days). Nothing is deleted while it runs.
@{bullet}@{b}Undo copies@{ub}: memory only; see
@{" Paths, merging and undo " link rules}.

@{h2}How big it can grow

@{bullet}A document: @{tt}max_doc_bytes@{utt} characters of compact JSON.
@{bullet}A namespace: @{tt}max_docs@{utt} documents.
@{bullet}Namespaces: no limit. Every session that uses the store without a
namespace adds one.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tool

The description and parameters come from the plugin's
@{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}. It tells the model to send JSON as
@{tt}data@{utt}, to let the plugin merge instead of typing the document
again, to use undo to recover from a mistake, and that other agents'
documents are read-only. Refusals come back with the texts in the other
nodes; the model can correct its call from them -- unknown names and keys
come with a "did you mean".

The chat's status line says what each call did: "wrote 'plan' (412 chars)",
"merged 'doc_3f9a1c07' → 'plan' [steps] (980 chars)", "set
'plan'.steps[0] (430 chars)", "deleted 2 key(s) from 'plan', 1 not found",
"undid merge → 'plan' (430 chars) (4 undo(s) left)"; a refusal shows as
failed: "merge 'plan' failed: ...".

@{h2}Tokens and cache

The plugin exists to save tokens: merges, copies and single values do not
pass through the model, and write answers carry only a summary. A
@{tt}read@{utt} returns the whole document, or the part at
@{tt}path@{utt}, indented -- up to @{tt}max_read_chars@{utt} (50000
characters by default); a longer one is refused with a hint to read a part. Read
parts, or look at @{tt}outline@{utt} first, when documents are large. Answers are appended to the history like any other
tool answer; the plugin never changes an earlier message or the system
prompt.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}A namespace is only a name: any agent of any user served by the
same store that passes it reads the documents. Documents without a
namespace are hidden only behind the session ID: passed as namespace, it
opens them too.
@{bullet}A text sent as @{tt}data@{utt} is stored as a text; the JSON it
may contain is not parsed.
@{bullet}The settings are read once; a change takes effect after a restart.
@endnode
